  Summary

  Converted all 4 .launch.py files to C++ executables — since ROS2 has no
  native C++ launch system, each one is a small C++ program that reproduces
  exactly what its Python launch file did (same nodes, same parameters, same
  startup delays), but runs as ros2 run instead of ros2 launch:

  | Original launch file | New C++ executable | |---|---| |
  mearm_hand_tracking/launch/mearm_hand_tracking.launch.py |
  mearm_hand_tracking_launch | |
  mearm_object_detection/launch/mearm_object_detection.launch.py |
  mearm_object_detection_launch | | mearm_description/launch/gazebo.launch.py
  | mearm_gazebo_launch | | mearm_description/launch/display.launch.py |
  mearm_display_launch |

  All three packages built cleanly.

  New commands to run everything in C++:

    source /opt/ros/lyrical/setup.bash
    source ~/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash
    
    # Gazebo simulation (replaces: ros2 launch mearm_description gazebo.launch.py)
    ros2 run mearm_description mearm_gazebo_launch
    
    # RViz-only visualization, no Gazebo (replaces: ros2 launch mearm_description display.launch.py)
    ros2 run mearm_description mearm_display_launch
    
    # Hand tracking (replaces: ros2 launch mearm_hand_tracking mearm_hand_tracking.launch.py)
    ros2 run mearm_hand_tracking mearm_hand_tracking_launch
    
    # Object detection (replaces: ros2 launch mearm_object_detection mearm_object_detection.launch.py)
    ros2 run mearm_object_detection mearm_object_detection_launch

  Each launcher accepts the same override arguments as its Python counterpart
  (e.g. camera_index:=1, params_file:=/path/to/file.yaml, model_path:=...),
  passed the same way: ros2 run <pkg> <launcher> camera_index:=1.

